		},
		{
			"id": "XolaRiyU",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 5772.24163907173,
			"y": 577.048646037734,
			"width": 1011.998779296875,
			"height": 350,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "dashed",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b5u",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 403674924,
			"version": 93,
			"versionNonce": 843032364,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783375806055,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "1. Contiguous Structures (Arrays / ArrayLists)\n- Execution: The traversal relies on pointer arithmetic. Because memory is contiguous, the \n  system calculates the exact address of the next element by adding a fixed offset (the size\n  of the data type) to the current index.\n- Hardware Impact: High cache locality. The CPU pre-fetches the sequential blocks into its \n  high-speed cache, resulting in highly efficient iteration.\n\n2. Non-Contiguous Structures (Linked Lists)\n- Execution: The traversal relies on pointer hopping (or dereferencing). The program reads \n  the next reference property of the current node to discover the arbitrary heap memory \n  address of the subsequent node.\n- Hardware Impact: Poor cache locality. Because nodes are scattered across the heap, \n  following references causes frequent CPU cache misses, forcing slower reads from main \n  RAM.",
			"rawText": "1. Contiguous Structures (Arrays / ArrayLists)\n- Execution: The traversal relies on pointer arithmetic. Because memory is contiguous, the \n  system calculates the exact address of the next element by adding a fixed offset (the size \n  of the data type) to the current index.\n- Hardware Impact: High cache locality. The CPU pre-fetches the sequential blocks into its \n  high-speed cache, resulting in highly efficient iteration.\n\n2. Non-Contiguous Structures (Linked Lists)\n- Execution: The traversal relies on pointer hopping (or dereferencing). The program reads \n  the next reference property of the current node to discover the arbitrary heap memory \n  address of the subsequent node.\n- Hardware Impact: Poor cache locality. Because nodes are scattered across the heap, \n  following references causes frequent CPU cache misses, forcing slower reads from main \n  RAM.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": "HCGAqJmB",
			"originalText": "1. Contiguous Structures (Arrays / ArrayLists)\n- Execution: The traversal relies on pointer arithmetic. Because memory is contiguous, the \n  system calculates the exact address of the next element by adding a fixed offset (the size \n  of the data type) to the current index.\n- Hardware Impact: High cache locality. The CPU pre-fetches the sequential blocks into its \n  high-speed cache, resulting in highly efficient iteration.\n\n2. Non-Contiguous Structures (Linked Lists)\n- Execution: The traversal relies on pointer hopping (or dereferencing). The program reads \n  the next reference property of the current node to discover the arbitrary heap memory \n  address of the subsequent node.\n- Hardware Impact: Poor cache locality. Because nodes are scattered across the heap, \n  following references causes frequent CPU cache misses, forcing slower reads from main \n  RAM.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "6DfagzTk",
