		{
			"id": "ERTjRbpS",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 6869.821653978772,
			"y": 300.13399665185295,
			"width": 1184.3389892578123,
			"height": 425,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b64",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 790408468,
			"version": 199,
			"versionNonce": 21281172,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783377011599,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "1. The Missing Patterns (What to go back and learn)\n\nBefore you move on to entirely new data structures, you need to grab the algorithmic techniques\nspecifically designed for Linked Lists and Arrays. \n\nProfessors love testing these:\n    1. The \"Two-Pointer\" Technique: This is an essential algorithm pattern. You use two pointers moving \n       through a list at the same time to solve problems in O(n) time without using extra memory.\n        - Example: How do you find the middle of a Linked List in one pass? (Answer: A \"slow\" pointer moves\n          one step, a \"fast\" pointer moves two steps. When the fast pointer hits null, the slow pointer is \n          in the exact middle).\n    2. Floyd’s Cycle Detection (Tortoise and Hare): What happens if a Linked List is broken and the tail \n       points back to the middle, creating an infinite loop? You need to know how to detect this \n       mathematically using the two-pointer technique.\n    3. Dynamic Array Resizing (Under the Hood): You know when to use an ArrayList vs a Linked List, but do \n       you know the math of how an ArrayList resizes? You should understand the O(n) penalty that occurs \n       when a dynamic array runs out of contiguous memory and has to copy itself into a new, larger block.",
			"rawText": "1. The Missing Patterns (What to go back and learn)\n\nBefore you move on to entirely new data structures, you need to grab the algorithmic techniques specifically designed for Linked Lists and Arrays. \n\nProfessors love testing these:\n    1. The \"Two-Pointer\" Technique: This is an essential algorithm pattern. You use two pointers moving \n       through a list at the same time to solve problems in O(n) time without using extra memory.\n        - Example: How do you find the middle of a Linked List in one pass? (Answer: A \"slow\" pointer moves \n          one step, a \"fast\" pointer moves two steps. When the fast pointer hits null, the slow pointer is \n          in the exact middle).\n    2. Floyd’s Cycle Detection (Tortoise and Hare): What happens if a Linked List is broken and the tail \n       points back to the middle, creating an infinite loop? You need to know how to detect this \n       mathematically using the two-pointer technique.\n    3. Dynamic Array Resizing (Under the Hood): You know when to use an ArrayList vs a Linked List, but do \n       you know the math of how an ArrayList resizes? You should understand the O(n) penalty that occurs \n       when a dynamic array runs out of contiguous memory and has to copy itself into a new, larger block.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "1. The Missing Patterns (What to go back and learn)\n\nBefore you move on to entirely new data structures, you need to grab the algorithmic techniques specifically designed for Linked Lists and Arrays. \n\nProfessors love testing these:\n    1. The \"Two-Pointer\" Technique: This is an essential algorithm pattern. You use two pointers moving \n       through a list at the same time to solve problems in O(n) time without using extra memory.\n        - Example: How do you find the middle of a Linked List in one pass? (Answer: A \"slow\" pointer moves \n          one step, a \"fast\" pointer moves two steps. When the fast pointer hits null, the slow pointer is \n          in the exact middle).\n    2. Floyd’s Cycle Detection (Tortoise and Hare): What happens if a Linked List is broken and the tail \n       points back to the middle, creating an infinite loop? You need to know how to detect this \n       mathematically using the two-pointer technique.\n    3. Dynamic Array Resizing (Under the Hood): You know when to use an ArrayList vs a Linked List, but do \n       you know the math of how an ArrayList resizes? You should understand the O(n) penalty that occurs \n       when a dynamic array runs out of contiguous memory and has to copy itself into a new, larger block.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
